<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James I of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1208–<a href="/years/1276">1276</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as James the Conqueror</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James I, called the Conqueror, was king of Aragon and count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from 1213 to 1276. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1229 and the Muslim kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> in 1238, creating new realms with their own laws within the Crown of Aragon. In the Treaty of Corbeil of 1258 he gave up most claims north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. His chronicle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llibre-dels-fets">Book of Deeds</a>, is the first royal autobiography in Catalan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon.jpg" alt="James I of Aragon" width="162" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vidal de Canellas y cancillería real del rey de Aragón.; derivative work by Escarlati · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaime_I_de_Arag%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>James I</b> the Conqueror left an account of his own reign, the Llibre dels fets, one of the earliest autobiographies by a medieval Christian king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Catalan nobles and towns contributed to <b>James I</b>’s conquest of Majorca in 1229, and the royal shipyards at Barcelona, the Drassanes, built the galleys that made the Crown a leading naval power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>James I of Aragon</b> signed the Treaty of Almizra with the Castilian heir Alfonso, fixing the frontier between their conquests in al-Andalus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-26">26 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King Louis IX of France and King <b>James I of Aragon</b> signed the Treaty of Corbeil.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-11">11 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating the Moors of the taifa of Balansiya, King <b>James I of Aragon</b> entered the city of Valencia, an event regarded as the dawn of the Kingdom of Valencia, a component realm of the Crown of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>James I of Aragon</b>'s army fought the Almohad troops holding Mallorca at Portopí, between Santa Ponsa and the City of Majorca, in the second major battle of his campaign to conquer the island.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-12">12 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Montpellier, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.61,3.88&amp;year=1252" aria-label="Montpellier on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4643 618 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James I of Aragon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5093 888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5093 888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.61,3.88&amp;year=1252">What was here in 1252? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
